WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.470
Watching a movie with friends and loved ones
can enrich the experience for everyone.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.190
But, as with all things, there are exceptions.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:11.940
Trust us, you won't want someone sitting next
to you while any of these movies are on.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.180
Her is a film about a lonely dude who buys
a sophisticated operating system that contains

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.630
a charming artificial intelligence, and then
promptly falls in love with her.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.340
It's quite literally about a man who falls
in love with a screen.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.220
In that way, there's something absolutely
perfect about watching it by yourself, particularly

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:33.450
if you're feeling a tad lonely, just for the
sheer satirical value of it all.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:38.079
Beyond that, though, there's something more
to Her that begs to be considered in a solitary,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:38.620
quiet way.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.560
Some people see the film as more of a comedy
mocking our relationship to technology.

00:00:42.569 --> 00:00:47.199
Others, meanwhile, see it as a very sincere
study of our relationship to tech, and to

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:47.820
each other.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:52.420
Either way, it's a fascinating film full of
questions you'd rather not have a friend asking

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.200
aloud in your ear while you watch.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:00.580
Alex Garland's gorgeous yet disturbing sci-fi
masterpiece was one of the most underseen

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:05.340
but fascinating films of 2018, and now that
it's available to watch at home, you're in

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.320
a prime position to watch it in the dark,
by yourself, absorbing every little detail

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.750
of the Shimmer and the weird way it seeps
into the brains of the film's characters.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.300
Annihilation is a film rich in visual and
thematic textures.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.433
It's a treatise on trauma and guilt, an exploration
of what it means to be an individual, and

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.220
a glimpse at what happens when we finally
get the transformation we so desperately seek.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.080
It's also a film that leaves the answers to
the questions it ponders somewhat ambiguous,

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:36.320
and while it can be fun to discuss theories
with people the moment the movie is over,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.390
Annihilation is a story that rewards silent
contemplation.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.100
It's a film that begs you not only to watch
it intently, but to sit with it in silence

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.960
for a while after the credits have rolled.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.100
With the right audience, the original Twin
Peaks series can be a blast to watch in a

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:51.600
group.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.460
It's quirky, it's funny, it's scary without
being overwhelming, and it's full of fun little

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.010
character details and quotes you can all talk
about later.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.620
"Entering the town of Twin Peaks.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.640
It's 5 miles south of the Canadian border,
12 miles west of the state line.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.560
I've never seen so many trees in my life."

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:14.020
Fire Walk With Me, the prequel film that followed
the original show's cancellation, is a different

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:14.520
beast.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.300
Directed by co-creator David Lynch without
his Twin Peaks creative partner Mark Frost,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.820
Fire Walk With Me abandons many of the more
comedic and lighthearted elements of the series

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:28.480
in favor of the bleak, brutal, absolutely
nightmarish story of the last week of Laura

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.240
Palmer's life.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.420
It's often a hard film to watch, and it expands
the Peaks mythology in ways that are often

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.760
hard to grasp upon first viewing, even with
that feature-length collection of deleted

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.760
scenes to expand the series' lore.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:45.420
Watching it with a raucous crowd of Twin Peaks
fans might not work, so, instead, watch it

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.920
quietly and absorb the many mysteries of a
place both wonderful and strange.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.480
Even at their zaniest, the films of the Coen
brothers are all marked by the duo's detail-oriented

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.660
and deeply intellectual signature style.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.780
That makes nearly all of them challenging
in some way, but Barton Fink might be their

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.500
most cerebral effort ever.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.000
The surreal story of a gifted playwright who
heads out to Hollywood in an attempt to bring

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:13.700
a degree of seriousness and meaning to motion
pictures, the film is an existential examination

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:18.600
of creative self-seriousness, which the film
itself both embraces and pokes fun at.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.780
"Strange as it may seem, Charlie, I guess
I write about people like you: the average

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.260
working stiff, the common man."

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.540
"Well, ain't that a kick in the head."

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.620
The film's themes, dialogue, and visual symbolism
all mirror the process of Fink's own confidence

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:38.520
and intellectual prowess going up in flames,
and what a glorious fire it is!

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.460
Watching the film alone both enhances the
sense of isolation you share with Fink as

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.860
a viewer, and underlines the cerebral loneliness
at play.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.920
Like the films of The Coen brothers, the films
of Paul Thomas Anderson are often overwhelmingly

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.890
cerebral, rich with a meaning that isn't always
obvious but is always compelling enough to

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.650
make a careful viewer lean forward and take
notice.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.890
The Master, perhaps more than any of his other
work, deliberately grabs at viewers' attention

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:08.180
with a tale about a charismatic leader, played
by the late Philip Seymour Hoffman, and the

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.629
curious convert who joins his cause.

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:12.220
"You shouldn't work in your condition."

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:13.080
"No, I can work."

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:14.780
"You're aberrated."

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:15.780
"No, I'm not."

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.220
"Know what that means?"

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:18.960
"No."

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.760
Comparisons to L. Ron Hubbard and Scientology
are obvious on the surface, but whatever The

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.550
Master is searching for in its own tense,
fascinating way runs much deeper than a thinly

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.660
veiled allegory for a real life belief system.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:38.520
In Anderson's hands, the film becomes a meditation
on manhood, faith, self-destruction, longing,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.780
fear, and raw human endurance.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.260
These are heavy things in a heavy movie, and
they're really worth sitting with alone, particularly

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:50.280
if the people you typically share your couch
with are the type to ask too many questions

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.350
the film itself isn't willing to answer.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:58.920
"If we meet again in the next life, you will
be my sworn enemy, and I will show you no

00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.249
mercy."

